---
import SectionHeader from "./SectionHeader.astro";
import { COUNTS, DIALECT, DOCS } from "../config";
import { gated, SERVERS } from "../data/servers";

/**
 * Every count on this page is derived from `data/servers.ts`, which `make
 * servers` generates from the manifest and `make servers-check` verifies in CI.
 * A typed "nine" here would be wrong on the day a tenth lands, in the one copy
 * of the number a visitor actually reads.
 *
 * The count is a claim about the CATALOG, not about what any install runs — the
 * list is the user's now and starts empty. The copy below has to keep saying
 * which one it means, because "nine servers" read as "nine servers you are
 * stuck with" is exactly the impression this section used to give on purpose.
 */
const gateIds = new Set(gated.map((s) => s.id));
---

<section id="servers" class="wrap scroll-mt-20 py-[96px]">
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start gap-16">
    <SectionHeader
      eyebrow="Servers"
      heading={`${COUNTS.serversTitle} to start with. Add your own.`}
    >
      <p class="m-0 text-[16px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
        Bastion ships with nothing installed. The {COUNTS.serversWord} below are a catalog you
        install from; anything else on npm you add by package name. Each one is fetched on demand
        into Bastion's own directory and run with the Node runtime in the app.
      </p>
      <p class="m-0 text-[14px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-muted text-pretty">
        What stays closed is how a request picks one. A client names a profile and a server id —
        never a path, never a command line. The child inherits your credentials and runs
        unsandboxed, so "run whatever the config names" is the same shape of hole as
        CVE-2025-49596, and it is the half worth keeping. <a
          href={DOCS.servers}
          rel="noopener"
          class="text-accent-hi hover:text-plate-top">The reference</a
        > has the argument in full.
      </p>
    </SectionHeader>

    <div class="flex min-w-[min(420px,100%)] flex-1 basis-[520px] flex-col gap-4">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
        <div
          class="flex min-w-[min(250px,100%)] flex-1 basis-[250px] flex-col gap-3 rounded-lg border border-accent/22 px-8 py-7.5"
          style="background:linear-gradient(140deg,rgb(255 208 138/9%),rgb(255 255 255/3%))"
        >
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
            <span
              class="font-display text-[62px] leading-[0.82] font-semibold tracking-[-0.05em]"
              style="background:linear-gradient(180deg,#FFD08A,#F2895C);-webkit-background-clip:text;background-clip:text;color:transparent"
            >
              {COUNTS.gated}
            </span>
            <span class="text-[13.5px] text-fg-muted">behind a write gate</span>
          </div>
          <p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
            Off until a profile turns it on. <code class="font-mono text-[13.5px]"
              >lab/unifi-network</code
            > with writes on beside <code class="font-mono text-[13.5px]">home/unifi-network</code> with
            them off is expressible; a process boundary never made it so.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="flex min-w-[min(250px,100%)] flex-1 basis-[250px] flex-col gap-3 rounded-lg border border-white/9 bg-white/[3.5%] px-8 py-7.5"
        >
          <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
            <span
              class="font-display text-[62px] leading-[0.82] font-semibold tracking-[-0.05em] text-fg/85"
            >
              {COUNTS.readOnly}
            </span>
            <span class="text-[13.5px] text-fg-muted">read-only</span>
          </div>
          <p class="m-0 text-[14.5px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-dim text-pretty">
            No mutating tool registered at all, so a host cannot call what it was never told about.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul class="m-0 grid list-none grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(190px,1fr))] gap-2 p-0">
        {
          SERVERS.map((s) => (
            <li class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2.5 rounded-[11px] border border-white/8 bg-white/3 px-3.5 py-3">
              <span class="font-mono text-[12.5px] text-fg/82" title={s.summary}>
                {s.id}
              </span>
              <span
                class:list={[
                  "font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[0.05em] whitespace-nowrap uppercase",
                  gateIds.has(s.id) ? "text-fg-muted" : "text-ok-ink",
                ]}
              >
                {gateIds.has(s.id) ? "write gate" : "read-only"}
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <p class="m-0 text-[13px] leading-[1.6] text-fg-muted text-pretty">
        None of the {COUNTS.serversWord} are modern — every one runs an SDK whose newest protocol is {
          DIALECT.children
        }, which is what they negotiate. The catalog said 2025-06-18 until a live handshake was run
        against one; that was Bastion's own pin masquerading as a fact about the servers. A server
        you add yourself is fronted the same way.{" "}
        <a href={DOCS.manifest} rel="noopener" class="text-accent-hi hover:text-plate-top">
          The catalog
        </a> is one file, and <a href={DOCS.servers} rel="noopener" class="text-accent-hi hover:text-plate-top">
          the reference table
        </a> has every variable each entry reads.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
